<file path=".jules/bolt.md">
## 2025-05-15 - [React Rendering Optimization]
**Learning:** In a chat application with frequent updates (typing indicators, status changes), rendering the entire message list is a major bottleneck. Memoizing message bubbles and calculations like date separator injection significantly improves UI responsiveness.
**Action:** Use `React.memo` for list items and `useMemo` for data processing logic that runs on every render.

## 2025-05-16 - [List Deduplication and Item Memoization]
**Learning:** Combining multiple chat streams (direct and group) using findIndex results in O(N²) complexity, which lags as the number of conversations grows. Memoizing Sidebar items is only effective when parent handlers and display helpers are stabilized via useCallback.
**Action:** Use Map for O(N) deduplication of combined lists and ensure all props passed to memoized list items have stable references.

## 2025-05-20 - [Prop Stability and Render Loop Memoization]
**Learning:** Passing large objects like `chatData` to memoized components (MessageBubble) causes frequent cache misses because these objects change on almost every database sync (e.g., `lastMessageAt`).
**Action:** Pass only required scalar values (e.g., `chatName`, `noForwards`) to memoized list items. Additionally, wrap the O(N) rendering loop in `useMemo` to skip the entire mapping process when unrelated state (like input text) changes.

## 2025-05-22 - [Centralizing Listeners for List Performance]
**Learning:** Initializing Firestore listeners or heavy state logic within hooks used by list items (like MessageBubble) creates an O(N) resource bottleneck. In a chat with 50 messages, this would open 50 redundant websocket connections/listeners.
**Action:** Centralize shared data (like user's owned sticker packs) into a single Context Provider. This reduces the resource footprint to O(1) and prevents cascading re-renders across the entire message list when shared state updates.
</file>

